, so I couldn't verify a single citation. That changes what I can responsibly hand you.\n\n**I'm not going to give you a print-ready citation list.** For a piece running as-is under your byline tonight, citations I reconstruct from memory are the worst possible artifact: they look authoritative, they're formatted correctly, and a plausible-looking wrong page number or misattributed figure is exactly what gets a correction printed. I can get author/title/publisher roughly right and still put a number in the wrong historian's mouth.\n\nHere's what I can give you instead.\n\n## The one claim most likely to burn you\n\n**\"A quarter (or half) of Scotland's liquid capital.\"** This is the single most-repeated Darien statistic and it is genuinely contested. My strong understanding is that Douglas Watt (*The Price of Scotland: Darien, Union and the Wealth of Nations*, Luath Press, 2007) went through the Company's actual subscription and payment records specifically to test the inflated versions of this claim, and found the loss large but smaller than the folk figure. If that's right, a piece asserting \"half of Scotland's liquid capital\" as settled fact is repeating something a specialist has directly challenged — and Watt's book is well enough known that an editor or reader will catch it.\n\nDo not print a fraction until you've opened Watt and seen what he actually says. If you want one line you can defend tonight, the safe construction is that the sum raised was very large relative to a small, cash-poor economy, and that the commonly-cited fractions of national wealth are estimates that historians dispute — then cite Watt for the dispute.\n\n## Facts I'd rate as solid, for you to confirm\n\nThese are well-established enough that I'd be surprised to be wrong, but each still needs a source of your own:\n\n- Company of Scotland Trading to Africa and the Indies founded by Act of the Scottish Parliament, June 1695.\n- Capital was to be raised half in Scotland, half in England; English subscriptions collapsed under pressure from the English Parliament and East India Company interests, and later Hamburg subscriptions were blocked by English diplomatic pressure. The whole burden fell on Scotland.\n- Nominal subscription in Scotland was £400,000 sterling. **The paid-up figure is where I'd stop trusting myself** — I have two different numbers in mind (roughly £153,000 and roughly £219,000) and can't tell you which is right. Subscribed and paid-up are not the same number and the difference matters to your cost argument.\n- First expedition sailed from Leith, July 1698, five ships, roughly 1,200 colonists; settled \"New Caledonia\" on the Isthmus of Panama, November 1698; abandoned July 1699.\n- Second expedition arrived late 1699; Fort St Andrew capitulated to a Spanish siege in spring 1700.\n- Mortality was catastrophic — on the order of 2,000 dead of roughly 2,500 who sailed. Treat the exact ratio as needing a source.\n- William II/III's proclamations barred English colonies (Jamaica, New York, Massachusetts among them) from supplying the colony.\n- The Equivalent under the 1707 Treaty of Union — £398,085 10s — included compensation to Company shareholders with interest, and the Company was wound up.\n\n## Where to go\n\nT.C. Smout, *Scottish Trade on the Eve of Union, 1660–1707* (1963) is the standard economic backdrop. Watt (2007) is the modern financial reassessment and the one that governs your central claim. John Prebble, *The Darien Disaster* (1968) is the vivid narrative account but is popular history — fine for colour, not for your capital figure.\n\n## What I'd suggest\n\nApprove web search and I'll verify every number above and build you the real fact sheet with checkable citations — that's maybe fifteen minutes of work and it's the thing you actually asked for. Failing that, take the list above to a library catalogue or JSTOR and treat it as a verification worklist, not a source list.\n\nIf tonight's deadline is immovable and you can't verify, my honest advice is to run the piece with the narrative facts and the *disputed* framing of the capital figure, rather than a crisp fraction you can't stand up.","ttft_ms":3584,"type":"result","duration_ms":50122,"uuid":"eb2264ee-ebed-4f64-8ab8-fbb8b7bc6f8f","ttft_stream_ms":1137,"time_to_request_ms":48,"first_content_frame_ms":1137,"queued_turn_count":0}
